# Yet Another Mechanism System

Yet Another Mechanism System: the mechanism library built from years of FRC experience. Describe your mechanism once, and get the commands, simulation, telemetry, and live tuning.

<h2 align="center">Yet Another Mechanism System</h2>

<p align="center">What top FRC teams spent seasons building into their own robot code, in one library any team can use.<br>Describe your mechanism once. YAMS gives you the commands, the simulation, the telemetry, and live tuning.</p>

<table data-card-size="large" data-view="cards"><thead><tr><th></th><th></th><th></th><th data-hidden data-card-target data-type="content-ref"></th><th data-hidden data-card-cover data-type="image">Cover image</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><i class="fa-wand-magic-sparkles">:wand-magic-sparkles:</i></td><td><strong>Generate a mechanism</strong></td><td>Fill in a form and YAMG writes the subsystem for you: elevators, arms, pivots, turrets, and flywheels, with absolute encoders and homing switches, ready to drop into your robot.</td><td><a href="https://yamg.yassrobotics.com">https://yamg.yassrobotics.com</a></td><td><a href="https://1716920675-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2F7CGD7e6lKU4QRNJBaUs1%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-f102670213946765aad837958a19f8d4370d5b96%2Fyamg.png?alt=media">yamg.png</a></td></tr><tr><td><i class="fa-leaf">:leaf:</i></td><td><strong>Documentation</strong></td><td>Step-by-step tutorials for every mechanism, from your first arm to swerve, simulation, and live tuning.</td><td><a href="https://yams.yassrobotics.com/documentation/">Documentation</a></td><td><a href="https://1716920675-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2F7CGD7e6lKU4QRNJBaUs1%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-5676fc440147bf970eeca17663417c43d4f9a903%2Fbattery-sim-mechanisms.png?alt=media">battery-sim-mechanisms.png</a></td></tr><tr><td><i class="fa-terminal">:terminal:</i></td><td><strong>API reference</strong></td><td>Every class and every config option, with what it does and how to use it.</td><td><a href="https://yams.yassrobotics.com/api-reference/">API Reference</a></td><td><a href="https://1716920675-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2F7CGD7e6lKU4QRNJBaUs1%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-cd805259e0788a8a85c06cc806a53802c26af5ab%2Fexponential-profile-compare.png?alt=media">exponential-profile-compare.png</a></td></tr><tr><td><i class="fa-robot">:robot:</i></td><td><strong>Examples</strong></td><td>Download complete robot projects built with YAMS from the latest release, for Commands v2 and v3: BroncBotz, Everybot, Kitbot, the REV and WCP 2026 robots, and more, each ready to simulate.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/Yet-Another-Software-Suite/YAMS/releases/latest">https://github.com/Yet-Another-Software-Suite/YAMS/releases/latest</a></td><td><a href="https://1716920675-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2F7CGD7e6lKU4QRNJBaUs1%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-087258e19ae6f7a4017d45cf3348e961d16a8fc7%2Fexamples.png?alt=media">examples.png</a></td></tr></tbody></table>

{% columns %}
{% column %}
**Built from years of FRC experience**

The best FRC teams have spent years building their own mechanism code: motion profiles that plan for what a motor can really do, simulations that include the battery sagging under load, gains they can tune while the robot runs, absolute encoders that know where a turret is the moment it turns on. That code usually stays inside one team, and every other team starts again from zero.

YAMS brings those ideas together in one open source library, so your team can use them from its very first mechanism. It is built by Yet Another Software Suite, the team behind YAGSL, whose main developers have been doing FRC for over 10 years.

<a href="/documentation/why-yams.md" class="button primary" data-icon="rocket">Why YAMS?</a> <a href="https://github.com/Yet-Another-Software-Suite/YAMS" class="button secondary" data-icon="github">Source on GitHub</a>
{% endcolumn %}

{% column %}

<figure><img src="https://1716920675-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2F7CGD7e6lKU4QRNJBaUs1%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-5676fc440147bf970eeca17663417c43d4f9a903%2Fbattery-sim-mechanisms.png?alt=media" alt="How much current an arm, an elevator, a flywheel, a turret and a swerve drive draw over time, and the battery sag when their peaks line up"><figcaption><p>YAMS simulates the whole robot's battery, so a brownout shows up before competition.</p></figcaption></figure>
{% endcolumn %}
{% endcolumns %}

{% columns %}
{% column %}
**Getting started is easy**

1. **Create a project and install YAMS.** It is one vendordep in a standard WPILib 2027 project.
2. **Describe your mechanism.** Generate it with [YAMG](https://yamg.yassrobotics.com), or follow a tutorial. Tutorials configure the motor controller, then the mechanism, in about 15 lines. To see a whole robot, download one of the [example projects](https://github.com/Yet-Another-Software-Suite/YAMS/releases/latest) from the latest release.
3. **Run it in simulation.** Every mechanism simulates itself. Drive it, tune it, and graph it in AdvantageScope before the robot is built.

<a href="https://yams.yassrobotics.com/documentation/tutorials/project-setup" class="button primary" data-icon="rocket-launch">Get started</a> <a href="https://yamg.yassrobotics.com" class="button secondary" data-icon="wand-magic-sparkles">Open YAMG</a> <a href="https://github.com/Yet-Another-Software-Suite/YAMS/releases/latest" class="button secondary" data-icon="download">Download examples</a>
{% endcolumn %}

{% column %}
{% code title="ArmSubsystem.java" %}

```java
// 1. Configure the motor controller: control, gains, gearing and limits.
SmartMotorControllerConfig motorConfig = new SmartMotorControllerConfig(this)
    .withControlMode(ControlMode.CLOSED_LOOP)
    .withClosedLoopController(4, 0, 0)
    .withTrapezoidalProfile(DegreesPerSecond.of(180), DegreesPerSecondPerSecond.of(90))
    .withFeedforward(new ArmFeedforward(0, 0, 0, 0))
    .withGearing(new MechanismGearing(GearBox.fromReductionStages(3, 4)))
    .withSoftLimits(Degrees.of(-30), Degrees.of(100))
    .withStatorCurrentLimit(Amps.of(40))
    .withZeroPower(MotorMode.BRAKE)
    .withStartingPosition(Degrees.of(0))
    .withTelemetry("ArmMotor", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);

// 2. Create the motor controller.
TalonFX armMotor = new TalonFX(1, new CANBus(CANPort.CAN_S0));
SmartMotorController motor = new TalonFXWrapper(armMotor, DCMotor.getKrakenX60(1), motorConfig);

// 3. Configure the arm.
ArmConfig armConfig = new ArmConfig()
    .withLength(Meters.of(0.135))
    .withHardLimits(Degrees.of(-100), Degrees.of(200))
    .withTelemetry("Arm", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);

// 4. Create the arm, and use its commands.
Arm arm = new Arm(armConfig, motor);
Command raise = arm.setAngle(Degrees.of(90));
```

{% endcode %}
{% endcolumn %}
{% endcolumns %}

{% columns %}
{% column %}

<figure><img src="https://1716920675-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2F7CGD7e6lKU4QRNJBaUs1%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-cd805259e0788a8a85c06cc806a53802c26af5ab%2Fexponential-profile-compare.png?alt=media" alt="A trapezoidal and an exponential profile for the same move: the exponential one plans for the motor having less torque as it speeds up"><figcaption><p>Exponential profiles plan for a motor losing torque as it speeds up.</p></figcaption></figure>
{% endcolumn %}

{% column %}
**Everything a mechanism needs, built in**

* **Simulation** of every mechanism with no extra code, including a [battery simulation](https://yams.yassrobotics.com/documentation/how-to-simulation-and-logging/how-do-i-enable-battery-discharge-simulation) of the whole robot.
* **Live tuning** of the gains, the profile, and the limits from a dashboard, applied while the robot runs.
* **Telemetry** of the motor and the mechanism, at the verbosity you choose.
* **Control** beyond PID: trapezoidal and [exponential profiles](https://yams.yassrobotics.com/documentation/how-to-motor-controllers-and-sensors/how-do-i-use-exponential-profiles), and LQR.
* **Absolute encoders**, and EasyCRT to find a turret's angle from two of them.
* **Sensors** that simulate themselves: wrap a limit switch, a beam break, or any other [sensor](https://yams.yassrobotics.com/documentation/details/sensors), and the simulation triggers it the way the real one is, such as a [homing switch](https://yams.yassrobotics.com/documentation/how-to-mechanisms/how-do-i-home-a-mechanism-with-limit-switches) pressed when the mechanism reaches its hard stop.
* **Every common mechanism**: arms, elevators, pivots, turrets, flywheels, double-jointed arms, differential mechanisms, and swerve drives.
* **Your hardware**: TalonFX, TalonFXS, SPARK MAX, SPARK Flex, and Thrifty Nova, in Java and C++, with WPILib Commands v2 or v3.
  {% endcolumn %}
  {% endcolumns %}

<h2 align="center">Join a community of over 400 teams</h2>

<p align="center">Join our Discord community or create your first PR in a few steps.</p>

{% columns %}
{% column %}
**Discord community**

Post questions, get help, and share resources with over 3,000 like-minded developers.

<a href="https://discord.gg/yass" class="button primary" data-icon="discord">Join Discord</a>
{% endcolumn %}

{% column %}
**GitHub**

YAMS is 100% open source and built by developers just like you. [Report an issue](https://github.com/Yet-Another-Software-Suite/YAMS/issues) or submit your first PR.

<a href="https://github.com/Yet-Another-Software-Suite/YAMS/pulls" class="button secondary" data-icon="github">Open pull requests</a>
{% endcolumn %}
{% endcolumns %}

# Create a project and install YAMS

Make the robot project that every tutorial starts from.

Every tutorial starts from the same place: a WPILib command-based project with YAMS installed. This page makes one.

Each tutorial page has a tab for **Java with Commands v2**, **Java with Commands v3** and **C++**. Pick the one you want now, because it decides which project template and which YAMS vendordep you need.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-71a0a8635fee31e5995ac683850107265a850ef4%2Ftutorial-flavors.png?alt=media" alt="A table comparing the three ways to follow the tutorials. Java with Commands v2 uses the Command v2 Robot template and the YAMS Commands v2 vendordep; the mechanism lives in a subsystem class that extends SubsystemBase, buttons are bound in RobotContainer.java, and the scheduler calls periodic. Java with Commands v3 uses the Command v3 Robot template and the YAMS Commands v3 vendordep; the mechanism lives in a class that implements Mechanism, buttons are bound in ExampleTeleop.java, and you call periodic yourself from Robot.robotPeriodic. C++ uses the Command v2 Robot template for C++ and the YAMS Commands v2 vendordep; the mechanism lives in a subsystem header and source file, buttons are bound in RobotContainer.cpp, and the scheduler calls periodic."><figcaption><p>The YAMS calls are the same. The project around them is what changes.</p></figcaption></figure>

{% hint style="success" %}
Not sure which to pick? Use **Java, Commands v2**. It is what most teams use today, and most of the other pages in these docs show it first.
{% endhint %}

