Bring up the Visual Studio Code command palette with <kbd>Ctrl+Shift+P</kbd> and type "WPILib". Select **WPILib: Create a new project**.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fv8EXUEReWVEt3PaxTETf%2Fimage.png?alt=media&amp;token=3499f3d8-f076-4da0-9f96-234a5e0d684c" alt=""><figcaption></figcaption></figure>

This brings up the New Project Creator:

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2FOZxXhcwt1pKd9pSXMCrx%2Fimage.png?alt=media&amp;token=50e2e75a-f9c7-4c68-9762-b2aa48e996cf" alt=""><figcaption></figcaption></figure>

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Java, Commands v2" %}

1. Click on **Select a project type (Example or Template)**.
2. Select **Template**, then **Java**, then **Command v2 Robot**.
   {% endtab %}

{% tab title="Java, Commands v3" %}

1. Click on **Select a project type (Example or Template)**.
2. Select **Template**, then **Java**, then **Command v3 Robot**.
   {% endtab %}

{% tab title="C++" %}

1. Click on **Select a project type (Example or Template)**.
2. Select **Template**, then **C++**, then **Command v2 Robot**.
   {% endtab %}
   {% endtabs %}

Then, whichever you picked:

3. Click on **Select a new project folder** and choose where to keep the project.
4. Fill in **Project Name**.
5. Enter your **Team Number**, so that you can deploy to your robot.
6. Check **Enable Desktop Support**, so that you can run the simulation.

Filled in, it looks something like this. The screenshots on this page show the Java Commands v2 choice.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fmir5YcWV8yINfcSfsgwU%2Fimage.png?alt=media&amp;token=c9040032-eb3d-467c-8645-0fc71785902e" alt=""><figcaption></figcaption></figure>

