# Step 2: The arm

Describe the arm and create the Arm mechanism.

The `SmartMotorController` from step 1 can already spin the motor. An `Arm` adds what makes it an arm: a physics simulation, a drawing of the mechanism for your dashboard, and the commands and triggers you will use in step 3.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-9ac4402ed53c7caf15787f40f7a6245c3ef8a7c2%2Fmech-two-configs.png?alt=media" alt="A diagram. A mechanism config, which says what the mechanism is, such as its length, mass, hard stops and how it is drawn, and a SmartMotorControllerConfig, which says how its motor behaves, such as gearing, PID, feedforward, soft limits and current, both go into the mechanism class. The mechanism then drives the simulation&#x27;s physics and Mechanism2d, and the real robot&#x27;s commands and telemetry."><figcaption><p>Two configs go into one mechanism.</p></figcaption></figure>

