## 3. Bind the buttons

`whileTrue` runs a command for as long as its button is held, and cancels it on release. The default command then takes back over.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-7c76c5d4398a7182749b5d7da48804ab7e0c2b6e%2Ftutorial-elevator-buttons.gif?alt=media" alt="Animation of the four face buttons of a controller, the elevator, and the command that is running. With no button held the default command runs: hold the elevator at 0 meters. While A is held its command runs: hold the elevator at 0.5 meters. While B is held: hold the elevator at 1 meter. While X is held the elevator is driven at a 30 percent duty cycle with the closed loop off. Each time a button is released the default command takes back over."><figcaption><p>A held button owns the elevator. Let go, and the default command brings it back down.</p></figcaption></figure>

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Java, Commands v2" %}
{% code title="RobotContainer.java" %}

```java
// Hold a height while the bottom (A) or right (B) face button is held.
driverController.faceDown().whileTrue(elevator.run(Meters.of(0.5)));
driverController.faceRight().whileTrue(elevator.run(Meters.of(1)));
// Drive the elevator up or down while the left (X) or top (Y) face button is held.
driverController.faceLeft().whileTrue(elevator.set(0.3));
driverController.faceUp().whileTrue(elevator.set(-0.3));
```

