## Which command ends?

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-3fadf4560c9eaad8dd912329ba84d272841fdef5%2Fmech-commands.png?alt=media" alt="Three timelines starting when the command is scheduled. run(setpoint), also called setAngle or setHeight, runs until something else takes the mechanism and holds the setpoint the whole time. runTo(setpoint, tolerance) ends when the mechanism is within the tolerance, and then the default command takes over. set(dutycycle) and setVoltage(volts) are open loop with no PID, run until interrupted, and the closed loop controller comes back when they end."><figcaption><p>When each kind of command ends.</p></figcaption></figure>

The buttons here use the command that never ends, because a button that is held should keep holding the carriage. Use the one that ends when you need to know the carriage has arrived, such as in an autonomous sequence. [run() vs runTo() Commands](/documentation/understanding/run-vs-runto.md) covers the difference, and [How do I set an elevator's target height?](/documentation/how-to-mechanisms/how-to-set-an-elevators-target-height.md) has more examples.

# Step 1: The motor controller

Configure the SparkMax and wrap it in a SmartMotorController.

{% hint style="info" %}
This page assumes a project with YAMS and REVLib installed. If you do not have one yet, start at [Create a project and install YAMS](/documentation/tutorials/project-setup.md).
{% endhint %}

A `SmartMotorController` is how YAMS talks to a motor controller. It takes three things:

1. **The vendor's motor controller object**, here a `SparkMax`.
2. **The motor** that is plugged into it, here one NEO.
3. **A `SmartMotorControllerConfig`** that says what the motor controller should do: its control mode, its PID and feedforward gains, and the gearing between the motor and the flywheel.

YAMS applies the config to the `SparkMax` for you, and uses the same config to build the simulation.

{% hint style="success" %}
**In a hurry?** Jump to [the complete file](#the-complete-file), copy it, and change the values called out under **For your robot**. The numbered parts explain each piece in a sentence or two. You do not need to understand all of it to get moving: the pages under **Understanding** go deeper when you want them.
{% endhint %}

## Create the file

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Java, Commands v2" %}
Create `ShooterSubsystem.java` in the `subsystems` folder, next to the template's `ExampleSubsystem.java`.
{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Java, Commands v3" %}
Create `ShooterMechanism.java` in the `mechanisms` folder, next to the template's `ExampleMechanism.java`.

The config comes from `yams.commands3.config`, and it takes the `Mechanism` where Commands v2 passes the subsystem.
{% endtab %}

{% tab title="C++" %}
Create `ShooterSubsystem.hpp` in `src/main/include/subsystems` and `ShooterSubsystem.cpp` in `src/main/cpp/subsystems`.

YAMS keeps a pointer to the config and to the `SparkMax`, so in C++ both are members of the subsystem. The constructor fills in the config and then creates the `SmartMotorController`, which is why that one is a `std::optional`.

`WithFeedback` is the C++ name of `withClosedLoopController`, and `WithMotorGearing` of `withGearing`.
{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

## 1. Start the config

Everything the motor controller should do goes into one `SmartMotorControllerConfig`. Start it, and put it in closed loop mode so that it can hold a speed. A flywheel holds a speed and has no position to protect, so this config has no motion profile, no starting position and no soft limits. It coasts at zero power, so that the wheel is not braked to a stop every time a command ends.

