## 6. Update both every loop

`updateTelemetry()` on each wheel publishes its telemetry, and `simIterate()` on each runs its simulation.

In Commands v2 and C++ the scheduler calls a subsystem's periodic methods for you, so there is nothing more to do.

In Commands v3 nothing calls a `Mechanism` every loop, so `Robot.java` does it. `Robot.java` has the two functions you need:

* **`robotPeriodic()`** runs every loop, on the robot and in simulation. Call the mechanism's `periodic()` here, before `scheduler.run()`, so that the commands see fresh values.
* **`simulationInit()`** runs once, when the simulation starts. The template does not have it yet, so add it, and start the mechanism's `simulationPeriodic()` here with `startSimulation(...)`, which runs it in its own `Notifier` every loop, in simulation only. That keeps the simulation from adding to the robot loop's time, as [Where the tutorials put things](/documentation/tutorials/project-setup.md#where-the-tutorials-put-things) explains.

Every mechanism you add to the robot gets one line in each of the two.

Two smaller changes tidy up the template while you are here. Keep `Scheduler.getDefault()` in a `scheduler` attribute instead of asking for it every loop, and publish the scheduler with `Telemetry.log(...)` after it runs, so that a dashboard shows which commands are running and which mechanisms they hold.

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Java, Commands v2" %}
{% code title="subsystems/DoubleFlywheelSubsystem.java" %}

```java
@Override
public void periodic() {
  // Publishes each flywheel's telemetry, and applies anything changed through live tuning.
  upperMotor.updateTelemetry();
  lowerMotor.updateTelemetry();
}

@Override
public void simulationPeriodic() {
  // Runs each flywheel's physics simulation.
  upperMotor.simIterate();
  lowerMotor.simIterate();
}
```

