# Step 3: Driving

Drive commands, a SwerveInputStream for the sticks, and the bindings.

`SwerveDrive` takes a chassis speed (forward, sideways and rotation) and sets all four modules for you.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-8314f33fa57f8b964ff714ce3dd6c1f50bb70505%2Ftutorial-swerve-modules.gif?alt=media" alt="Animation of two controller sticks and a top view of a swerve drive with its four modules. Left stick forward: every module points forward at the same speed. Left stick left: every module turns to point left and the robot does not rotate. Right stick left: the modules form a circle and the robot spins in place. Both sticks: each module gets its own angle and speed."><figcaption><p>You ask for a chassis speed. <code>SwerveDrive</code> works out the four modules.</p></figcaption></figure>

Three things are added in this step:

* **A drive command** that gives the drive field relative chassis speeds every loop.
* **`driveToPose(pose)`**, which uses the two PID controllers from step 2 to drive to a pose on the field.
* **A `SwerveInputStream`**, which turns the raw stick values into field relative chassis speeds, with the deadband, the cubing and the alliance flip already applied.

