These methods use `ChassisVelocities`, which is WPILib 2027's name for `ChassisSpeeds`. `lock()` turns every module toward the robot center, the X pattern, and holds it there.

{% hint style="info" %}
In simulation, `drive.getSimPose()` returns a ground truth `Pose2d`, separate from `getPose()`'s fused odometry estimate. It is a convenient "known truth" for a simulated vision subsystem to generate target detections from. It only advances during `simIterate()` and stays at the starting pose on a real robot.
{% endhint %}

{% hint style="success" %}
**Tuning the `driveToPose()` gains live**: when both the translation and the rotation controller are configured, YAMS publishes a tuning command and a tunable target pose. See [auto-align](/documentation/understanding/swerve-drive.md#auto-align) for how to use it.
{% endhint %}

## Simulate it

Run **WPILib: Simulate Robot Code**, connect a controller, and enable teleop. The drive publishes its own `Field2d`, so add the field widget to your dashboard and watch the robot move as you push the sticks.

