# How do I set an arm's target angle?

An `Arm`'s position setpoint is an `Angle` expressed in WPILib's typed unit system (`Angle`, `org.wpilib.units.measure.Angle`). Understanding the difference between `run()`, which tracks the setpoint continuously, and `runTo()`, which ends the command once the setpoint is reached within tolerance, shapes how you bind controls in `RobotContainer`.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-b9bf2f3b35bcc8a63582994010075c40a1e80efe%2Fhowto-arm-run-vs-runto.gif?alt=media" alt="Animation of the same arm moving to 45 degrees with two commands, with a default command of run to 0 degrees. With run the command keeps running after the arm arrives, and the arm stays at 45 degrees. With runTo and a tolerance of 3 degrees the command ends once the arm has stayed inside the tolerance for 0.1 seconds, then the default command takes over and moves the arm back to 0 degrees."><figcaption><p><code>run()</code> keeps running until something interrupts it. <code>runTo()</code> ends at the setpoint, and the default command takes over.</p></figcaption></figure>

