# How do I shoot with a shooter?

A flywheel shooter's effectiveness depends on reaching a stable velocity setpoint before the game piece enters the shooter. `FlyWheel` exposes a velocity trigger (`near`) so you can gate game piece injection on whether the wheel has spun up, rather than using a fixed time delay.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-b7c09583292da1d92654951d57a0bdc8f656350d%2Fhowto-shooter-at-speed.gif?alt=media" alt="Animation of a flywheel spinning up to 3000 RPM and shooting two balls, with a band of 100 RPM around the setpoint. Each shot drops the speed by several hundred RPM, and it overshoots and rings as it recovers. A lane for the at speed trigger is true inside the band, including for an instant each time the speed passes through it. A lane for the feeder, which uses the trigger with a 0.1 second debounce, is only true once the speed has stayed in the band, so the second ball is not fed until the wheel has settled."><figcaption><p>Each shot slows the wheel and it rings as it recovers. The tolerance band and a debounce keep the feeder off until it has settled.</p></figcaption></figure>

