# How do I use a DCMotor that isn't in WPILib?

WPILib ships physics models for common FRC motors (NEO, Falcon, CIM, etc.), but third-party or custom motors are not included. When your simulation needs accurate physics for an unlisted motor, you construct a `DCMotor` directly from its datasheet constants, stall torque, stall current, free speed, and free current, so the simulated plant behaves the same as your real hardware. Use this whenever you are simulating a mechanism driven by a motor that `DCMotor` does not already have a static factory for.

Sometimes the motor you use is not yet included in WPILib.

WPILib's `org.wpilib.math.system.DCMotor` has static factories for the motors it knows about (`getNEO`, `getNeo550`, `getNeoVortex`, `getFalcon500`, `getKrakenX60`, `getKrakenX44`, `getMinion`, and more). Each one is just a call to the `DCMotor` constructor, for example the Kraken X60:

```java
public static DCMotor getKrakenX60(int numMotors) {
  // From https://store.ctr-electronics.com/announcing-kraken-x60/
  return new DCMotor(
      12, 7.09, 366, 2, Units.rotationsPerMinuteToRadiansPerSecond(6000), numMotors);
}
```

Create a `DCMotor` from the datasheet values. Use ReCa.lc to calculate them.

{% embed url="<https://www.reca.lc/motors>" %}

```java
/**
 * Constructs a DC motor.
 *
 * @param nominalVoltage Voltage at which the motor constants were measured.
 * @param stallTorque Torque when stalled.
 * @param stallCurrent Current draw when stalled.
 * @param freeCurrent Current draw under no load.
 * @param freeSpeed Angular velocity under no load.
 * @param numMotors Number of motors in a gearbox.
 */
public DCMotor(
    double nominalVoltage,
    double stallTorque,
    double stallCurrent,
    double freeCurrent,
    double freeSpeed,
    int numMotors)
```

Torque is in newton-meters, current in amps and free speed in radians per second.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-61664746c59e9e832bb135864f3781085405ac18%2Fhowto-dcmotor-curve.png?alt=media" alt="A graph of torque and current against motor speed for a Minion at 12 volts. Torque falls in a straight line from the stall torque of 3.1 newton meters at zero speed to zero at the free speed of 7200 RPM. Current falls from the stall current of 200.46 amps to the free current of 1.43 amps. Beside it, the DCMotor constructor takes the nominal voltage, stall torque, stall current, free current, free speed and number of motors, in that order."><figcaption><p>The four datasheet values and where each goes in the constructor.</p></figcaption></figure>

For example, before WPILib added `DCMotor.getMinion()`, a [Minion motor](https://store.ctr-electronics.com/products/minion-brushless-motor) could be created with:

```java
import static org.wpilib.units.Units.RPM;
import static org.wpilib.units.Units.RadiansPerSecond;
import org.wpilib.math.system.DCMotor;

int numMotors = 1;
new DCMotor(12, 3.1, 200.46, 1.43, RPM.of(7200).in(RadiansPerSecond), numMotors);
```

# How do I sense activity from the motor current?

Current sensing lets your robot detect when a mechanism is stalling against a hard stop, for example, an elevator that has fully descended or a claw that has fully closed, without requiring a dedicated limit switch. Because `SmartMotorController` exposes stator current, you can wire a `Trigger` directly to a current threshold, which is useful when a sensor was never installed or when adding one mid-season is not practical.

There are situations where the mechanical team never confirmed with programming on what they would need to plan for and it is too late to redesign the mechanism and add a proper sensor.

In this case you can sense if there is a game piece by the current on the smart motor controller?

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-88ace0b2c092d5d2bc9bb39eab077676bfe86f18%2Fhowto-current-sensing.gif?alt=media" alt="Animation of a graph of stator current against a threshold of 40 amps. The current spikes over the threshold for a moment when the motor starts, runs at about 12 amps, then rises over the threshold and stays there when a game piece jams against the roller. The raw check is true during both. The debounced isGamePieceIn is false during the start up spike and turns true 0.1 seconds after the game piece arrives."><figcaption><p>The <code>Debouncer</code> ignores the short spike when the motor starts, and reports the game piece once the current has stayed over the threshold for 0.1 seconds.</p></figcaption></figure>

{% embed url="<https://docs.wpilib.org/en/stable/docs/software/advanced-controls/filters/debouncer.html#debouncer>" %}

