## The difference between rotor, mechanism, and measurement

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2FAMxbim1J0b5WuJ94m9ja%2Fimage.png?alt=media&amp;token=43ab64dc-75d5-4537-a62b-9fb48fed9a57" alt=""><figcaption></figcaption></figure>

* **Rotor** is the motor output shaft.
* **Mechanism** is the gearbox AND sprocket output shaft.
* **Measurement** is the distance that the **Mechanism** travelled via its [**Mechanism Circumference**](/documentation/how-to-motor-controllers-and-sensors/how-to-find-your-mechanism-circumference.md)**.**

{% hint style="info" %}
Cascading elevator **Mechanism** gearings should be divided by the stages in the Elevator!
{% endhint %}

An easy way to do this with YAMS is by using `GearBox.fromReductionStages`and `Sprocket.fromStages`!

## Configuring a motor

Everything about a motor is described by a `SmartMotorControllerConfig`: gearing, closed loop control, limits, encoders, followers and simulation. The [SmartMotorControllerConfig](/documentation/understanding/editor/config.md) pages explain every option with a graphic and an example, and say which motor controllers support it.

{% hint style="warning" %}
Not every option works on every motor controller. See [which options are not supported everywhere](/documentation/understanding/editor/config.md#not-every-option-works-on-every-motor-controller).
{% endhint %}

## Previous configurations

YAMS resets the motor controller's previous configuration by default, so settings left over from another run cannot turn into a headache. [`withResetPreviousConfig`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/motor-basics.md#withresetpreviousconfig) changes that.

## Power optimization is crucial to prevent brownouts

Power optimization can be done with [supply, and stator current limits, ramp rates](/documentation/understanding/editor/limiting-power-consumption.md), and adequately geared gearboxes and motors. In simulation, every mechanism's current draw feeds into one shared [Battery Simulation](/documentation/understanding/battery-simulation.md), so voltage sag from combined load, and, optionally, a battery discharging over a match, is realistic before you ever get to the field.

## Tuning our closed loop controllers

{% hint style="info" %}

## Motion profiles

The rule of thumb is:

1. Use trapezoid profile if your DC motor is always current-limited or you’re doing torque control
2. Use exponential profile if your DC motor is never current-limited
3. Use [the algorithm in this whitepaper](https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/whitepaper-trapezoidal-exponential-motion-profiling/443468/12?u=nstrike) if you’re sometimes current-limited (not implemented in YAMS, yet)
   {% endhint %}

Every `SmartMotorController` in YAMS supports motion profiled PID and feedforward, though not always in the same place: see [where the loop runs](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/closed-loop-control.md#where-the-loop-runs). The options are covered in [Closed loop control](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/closed-loop-control.md), [Feedforward](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/feedforward.md) and [Motion profiles](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/motion-profiles.md), and [Live tuning](/documentation/understanding/live-tuning.md) shows how to find the values.

Simulation never exactly matches real life, so you can set [separate PID and feedforward values for simulation only](/documentation/understanding/editor/simulation-only-pid-+-feedforward.md).

You can learn how to tune PID and feedforwards from WPILib here!

{% embed url="<https://docs.wpilib.org/en/stable/docs/software/advanced-controls/introduction/tuning-turret.html>" %}

## External encoders

YAMS accepts external encoders that the motor controller can read itself, which means the same vendor. [Encoders](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/encoders.md) covers attaching one, its gearing, its zero offset and which encoder the closed loop follows, and the [feature matrix](/documentation/understanding/editor/feature-matrix.md#external-encoders) lists the encoders each wrapper accepts.

{% hint style="danger" %}
Absolute encoders cannot go beyond 1 rotation or 360 degrees, even less with discontinuity points!

DO **NOT** USE AN ABSOLUTE ENCODER WITH A GEAR RATIO WITH AN INPUT GREATER THAN 1 UNLESS YOU KNOW WHAT YOU'RE DOING!
{% endhint %}

## Follower motors

There are two kinds of follower. **Tightly coupled** followers are motors on the same shaft or gearbox and use [`withFollowers`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/followers.md#withfollowers). **Loosely coupled** followers are motors that are not directly connected but must move together, and use [`withLooselyCoupledFollowers`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/followers.md#withlooselycoupledfollowers). [Followers](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/followers.md) has the full comparison.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-8fdf189e915651eb7bf985c14d6f3e6c63613df0%2Fsmc-followers.png?alt=media" alt="Two panels. Tightly coupled, withFollowers: a leader and a follower are on one shaft or gearbox and the follower copies the leader&#x27;s output. The motors are mechanically locked, there is one closed loop and one config, the followers are the same vendor as the leader, and voltage and duty cycle reach every motor. Loosely coupled, withLooselyCoupledFollowers: two separate motors joined only by the mechanism get the same setpoint and each runs its own PID. They are not mechanically locked, each has its own closed loop and config, vendors can be mixed, and voltage and duty cycle are not forwarded, so the leader moves alone."><figcaption><p>Tightly coupled followers share a shaft. Loosely coupled followers are separate motors.</p></figcaption></figure>

### Loosely coupled follower motors

Each loosely coupled follower is sent the leader's position and velocity setpoints, and reaches them with its own closed loop. A mechanism class works with them: give it the leader.

{% hint style="warning" %}
Loosely coupled followers are **not** sent voltage or duty cycle requests: they stop while the leader is driven at one. A follower whose closed loop runs on the SystemCore (a Thrifty Nova, a SPARK with an exponential profile, or anything with an LQR) only stops in YAMS releases after v2026.10.15. They are not sent `setEncoderPosition`, and are not updated with the leader: call `updateTelemetry()` and `simIterate()` on each one. [What reaches the followers](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/followers.md#what-reaches-the-followers) has the full list.
{% endhint %}

## You don't have to rely on YAMS to configure your SMC

YAMS is not the only way to configure your SmartMotorController! You can always fetch the config object, if available, from `SmartMotorController` like shown below

```java
(SparkFlex)smartMotorController.getMotorController();
(SparkFlexConfig)smartMotorController.getMotorControllerConfig();
```

## Some mechanism config options modify SMC configs

Mechanism options like `.withSoftLimits` often will call the `SmartMotorControllerConfig.withSoftLimits` and do nothing else. This is to provide a seamless configuration for relevant areas in different configs.

## Simulation without a mechanism

`SmartMotorController`s require `SmartMotorController.simIterate` to be called in `simulationPeriodic` of your subsystem to provide simulation IF your motor controller is not used by a mechanism. It also proper to include the `SmartMotorController.updateTelemetry` as well.

```java
  @Override
  public void periodic()
  {
    smartMotorController.updateTelemetry();
  }

  @Override
  public void simulationPeriodic()
  {
    smartMotorController.simIterate();
  }
```

## Passing a vendor config

`SmartMotorControllerConfig` can start from a vendor config that you supply, so vendor specific settings can be set alongside the YAMS options. See [`withVendorConfig`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/motor-basics.md#withvendorconfig).

# Followers

Two ways to have more than one motor move one mechanism: **tightly coupled** followers and **loosely coupled** followers.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-8fdf189e915651eb7bf985c14d6f3e6c63613df0%2Fsmc-followers.png?alt=media" alt="Two panels. Tightly coupled, withFollowers: a leader and a follower are on one shaft or gearbox and the follower copies the leader&#x27;s output. The motors are mechanically locked, there is one closed loop and one config, the followers are the same vendor as the leader, and voltage and duty cycle reach every motor. Loosely coupled, withLooselyCoupledFollowers: two separate motors joined only by the mechanism get the same setpoint and each runs its own PID. They are not mechanically locked, each has its own closed loop and config, vendors can be mixed, and voltage and duty cycle are not forwarded, so the leader moves alone."><figcaption><p>Tightly coupled followers share a shaft. Loosely coupled followers are separate motors.</p></figcaption></figure>

|                        | Tightly coupled: `withFollowers`                            | Loosely coupled: `withLooselyCoupledFollowers`                                   |
| ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| The motors are         | Mechanically locked on one shaft or gearbox                 | Separate, joined only by the mechanism                                           |
| Closed loop            | One, on the leader. Followers copy its output               | One on each motor, on its own encoder                                            |
| Config                 | Only the leader has one                                     | Every motor has its own                                                          |
| Vendors                | Same vendor as the leader                                   | Can be mixed                                                                     |
| Voltage and duty cycle | Reach every motor                                           | **Not forwarded.** The leader moves alone, and the followers stop                |
| Use it for             | Two motors on one gearbox, such as an elevator or a shooter | Two actuators that must arrive together, such as a cross bar lifted at both ends |
| Mechanism classes      | Work with them                                              | Work with them: give the mechanism the leader                                    |

## `withFollowers`

Tightly coupled followers, for motors on the same shaft or gearbox. Each follower copies the leader's output, so only the leader needs a config.

```java
.withFollowers(Pair.of(new SparkMax(CANPorts.fromBusId(1), 4, MotorType.kBrushless), false),
               Pair.of(new SparkMax(CANPorts.fromBusId(1), 5, MotorType.kBrushless), true))
```

Pass the vendor's motor object, not a `SmartMotorController`. The boolean is whether that follower is inverted relative to the leader: motors facing each other across a gearbox usually are.

**Supported on every motor controller.** Followers must be the same vendor as the leader. A TalonFX and a TalonFXS are both CTRE, so either can follow the other. To mix brands, use [`withLooselyCoupledFollowers`](#withlooselycoupledfollowers).

| TalonFX                          | TalonFXS                         | SPARK MAX / Flex       | Thrifty Nova          |
| -------------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ---------------------- | --------------------- |
| ✅ TalonFX and TalonFXS followers | ✅ TalonFXS and TalonFX followers | ✅ SPARK followers only | ✅ Nova followers only |

## `withLooselyCoupledFollowers`

Loosely coupled followers are for motors that are not on the same shaft or gearbox but must arrive at the same place: an intake arm driven from both sides, two linear actuators joined by a cross bar, or a shooter with a wheel on each end. Each follower is a full `SmartMotorController` with its own config, its own encoder and its own closed loop. The leader sends it every position and velocity setpoint it is given.

**You can mix brands.** Because each motor closes its own loop, a loosely coupled follower can be any supported motor controller, whatever the leader is, such as a SPARK leading a TalonFX.

**Supported on:** every supported motor controller.

### Use them with a mechanism class

A mechanism class such as `Arm`, `Elevator` or `FlyWheel` works with loosely coupled followers. Give the mechanism the leader, and name the followers in the leader's config. The mechanism's commands, such as `arm.setAngle(...)`, then move every motor.

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Java, Commands v2" %}
{% code title="subsystems/IntakeArmSubsystem.java" %}

```java
public class IntakeArmSubsystem extends SubsystemBase {

  // The right side of the arm. Declare a follower before the leader, which names it.
  private final SparkMax rightSpark = new SparkMax(CANPort.CAN_S0, 5, MotorType.kBrushless);
  private final SmartMotorController rightMotor = new SparkWrapper(rightSpark, DCMotor.getNEO(1),
      new SmartMotorControllerConfig(this)
          .withControlMode(ControlMode.CLOSED_LOOP)
          .withClosedLoopController(50, 0, 0)
          .withSimClosedLoopController(50, 0, 0)
          .withTrapezoidalProfile(DegreesPerSecond.of(90), DegreesPerSecondPerSecond.of(45))
          .withFeedforward(new ArmFeedforward(0, 0, 0))
          .withSimFeedforward(new ArmFeedforward(0, 0, 0))
          .withGearing(new MechanismGearing(GearBox.fromReductionStages(3, 4)))
          // The right motor faces the other way, so it is inverted here, in its own config.
          .withMotorInverted(true)
          .withZeroPower(MotorMode.BRAKE)
          .withStatorCurrentLimit(Amps.of(40))
          .withStartingPosition(Degrees.of(-5))
          .withSoftLimits(Degrees.of(-20), Degrees.of(30))
          .withTelemetry("IntakeArmRightMotor", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH));

  // The left side of the arm: the leader, which the Arm drives.
  private final SparkMax leftSpark = new SparkMax(CANPort.CAN_S0, 4, MotorType.kBrushless);
  private final SmartMotorController leftMotor = new SparkWrapper(leftSpark, DCMotor.getNEO(1),
      new SmartMotorControllerConfig(this)
          .withControlMode(ControlMode.CLOSED_LOOP)
          .withClosedLoopController(50, 0, 0)
          .withSimClosedLoopController(50, 0, 0)
          .withTrapezoidalProfile(DegreesPerSecond.of(90), DegreesPerSecondPerSecond.of(45))
          .withFeedforward(new ArmFeedforward(0, 0, 0))
          .withSimFeedforward(new ArmFeedforward(0, 0, 0))
          .withGearing(new MechanismGearing(GearBox.fromReductionStages(3, 4)))
          .withMotorInverted(false)
          .withZeroPower(MotorMode.BRAKE)
          .withStatorCurrentLimit(Amps.of(40))
          .withStartingPosition(Degrees.of(-5))
          .withSoftLimits(Degrees.of(-20), Degrees.of(30))
          .withTelemetry("IntakeArmLeftMotor", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH)
          // The right motor is sent every position and velocity setpoint the left motor is given.
          .withLooselyCoupledFollowers(rightMotor));

  private final Arm arm = new Arm(new ArmConfig()
      .withHardLimits(Degrees.of(-25), Degrees.of(35))
      .withLength(Meters.of(0.5))
      .withTelemetry("IntakeArm", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH),
      leftMotor);

  public Command setAngle(Angle angle) {
    // Moves both motors.
    return arm.setAngle(angle);
  }

  @Override
  public void periodic() {
    arm.updateTelemetry();
    // The Arm only updates the leader. Update each follower yourself.
    rightMotor.updateTelemetry();
  }

  @Override
  public void simulationPeriodic() {
    arm.simIterate();
    rightMotor.simIterate();
  }
}
```

{% endcode %}
{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Java, Commands v3" %}
The same as Commands v2, with `SmartMotorControllerConfig` and `Arm` from `yams.commands3`, and the class a `Mechanism`. A `Mechanism` has no periodic hook, so call `updateTelemetry()` on the arm and on each follower from `Robot.robotPeriodic()`, and run their `simIterate()` in a `Notifier`, started from `Robot.simulationInit()`, as in [Where the tutorials put things](/documentation/tutorials/project-setup.md#where-the-tutorials-put-things).
{% endtab %}

{% tab title="C++" %}
Create each follower before the leader, and name it in the leader's config:

```cpp
m_rightMotor.emplace(&m_rightSpark, wpi::math::DCMotor::NEO(1), &m_rightConfig);

m_leftConfig
    // ... config options
    .WithLooselyCoupledFollowers({&m_rightMotor.value()});  // the right motor is sent the left motor's setpoints

m_leftMotor.emplace(&m_leftSpark, wpi::math::DCMotor::NEO(1), &m_leftConfig);
```

Call `UpdateTelemetry()` and `SimIterate()` on each follower, as on the arm.
{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

### What reaches the followers

| The leader is given                                                                                                                 | The followers                                                                |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| A position or velocity setpoint, such as from `arm.setAngle(...)` or `flywheel.run(...)`                                            | Are sent the same setpoint, and each reaches it with its own closed loop     |
| A change from live tuning: gains, feedforward, motion profile, current limits, ramp rates, soft limits, gearing or closed loop slot | Are given the same change                                                    |
| A duty cycle of 0, which stops it                                                                                                   | Stop too\*                                                                   |
| Any other duty cycle or voltage, such as from `arm.set(...)`, `arm.setVoltage(...)` or SysId                                        | **Are not sent it.** The leader moves alone, and the followers are stopped\* |
| `setEncoderPosition(...)`, such as when zeroing or homing                                                                           | **Are not sent it.** Set each follower's encoder yourself                    |
| `updateTelemetry()` and `simIterate()`                                                                                              | **Are not updated.** Call them on each follower yourself                     |
| Its starting config                                                                                                                 | **Are not given it.** Each follower only has its own config                  |

\* A follower whose closed loop runs on the SystemCore only stops in YAMS releases after v2026.10.15. See the first pitfall below.

### Common pitfalls

* **The follower doesn't stop (YAMS v2026.10.15 and earlier).** A follower's closed loop runs on the SystemCore when it is a Thrifty Nova, a SPARK with an exponential profile, or any motor controller with an LQR. In YAMS v2026.10.15 and earlier, stopping the leader doesn't stop that loop, so the follower keeps driving to the last setpoint: on through `arm.set(0)`, and against the leader through `arm.set(...)`, `arm.setVoltage(...)` and SysId. Later releases stop it with the leader. Until you update, stop the follower's closed loop yourself while the leader is driven at a duty cycle or voltage:

  ```java
  arm.set(dutyCycle)
      .beforeStarting(() -> {
        rightMotor.stopClosedLoopController();
        rightMotor.setDutyCycle(0);
      })
      .finallyDo(rightMotor::startClosedLoopController);
  ```
* **The follower does nothing in simulation, and logs nothing.** Call `updateTelemetry()` and `simIterate()` on each follower, every loop.
* **The follower doesn't follow the profile.** The leader sends the follower the final setpoint, so a follower needs the same motion profile as the leader, and the same gearing, gains and feedforward, or it gets there at a different speed.
* **The follower drives the wrong way.** Each motor is inverted in its own config, with `withMotorInverted`. Motors on opposite sides of a mechanism usually face each other, so one of them is inverted.
* **The two sides fight.** Nothing keeps the motors together except the mechanism: each one only follows its own encoder. If they start at different positions, or one side slips, they pull against each other through the mechanism. Give every motor the same starting position, and when you zero or home the mechanism, set every motor's encoder.
* **Manual control moves only one side.** A voltage or duty cycle, such as manual control with a joystick or a SysId routine, only drives the leader, and the followers are stopped. Command each motor yourself, or characterize one side with the other side unpowered.

### Motors that need different setpoints

Loosely coupled followers are sent the leader's setpoints exactly. When the motors need different ones, such as two flywheels spinning at different speeds, command each `SmartMotorController` yourself: see [How do I control a mechanism without a mechanism class?](/documentation/how-to-mechanisms/how-do-i-control-a-mechanism-without-a-mechanism-class.md) and the [double flywheel tutorial](/documentation/tutorials/double-flywheel/spin-and-followers.md).

