## Known limitations

### SparkMax/SparkFlex (SparkWrapper)

* Exponential profiles run on the SystemCore, not on the motor controller
* Supply current limits are not supported
* External encoders must be REV-compatible (Through Bore, Hex)

### TalonFX/TalonFXS

* Requires Phoenix 6 library
* CANcoder/CANdi must be on same CAN bus for best performance

### Thrifty Nova (NovaWrapper)

* Requires ThriftyLib 2027
* All closed loop control, motion profiles and feedforwards run on the SystemCore
* One ramp rate for open and closed loop control
* The internal encoder cannot be inverted (`withEncoderInverted` throws an `IllegalArgumentException`)
* Vendor control requests (`withVendorControlRequest`) are not supported
* Hardware followers must be `Nova` controllers
* Setting the encoder velocity is only supported in simulation

# Sensors

YAMS Supports simulation really well, but my sensors don't.

## What is a `Sensor`?

A `Sensor` is a wrapper around what would be a real sensor on your robot, like a `CanandColor`, beam break, `CANRange`, `LaserCAN`, or anything else! While some of these have really good simulation capabilities; others have no simulation capabilities.

Sensors essentially report back primitive values. Sometimes we know the sensor should trip at a certain time during the match, like autonomous, or under certain conditions like game piece trade offs or intaking for a second or more. Sometimes we want to trigger the sensor ourselves too!

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-f1d9f87dc66eb6f8bc4725bcc486c062f97b655d%2Fsensor-value-source.png?alt=media" alt="Two panels. On the real robot, getAsBoolean returns the real sensor reading from the supplier you gave, and simulated values are ignored. In simulation, the first simulated value whose condition is true is returned. Otherwise the value shown in the Sim GUI is returned, which starts as the default and can be changed by hand or with getField and set."><figcaption><p>Your code makes the same call on the real robot and in simulation.</p></figcaption></figure>

## How do I create a `Sensor`?

{% hint style="info" %}
Using a `Sensor` wrapper will NEVER be as fast or efficient as using the original input.
{% endhint %}

You can create a sensor using `SensorConfig` which is where you define the Sensor name, and fields.

```java
import org.wpilib.hardware.discrete.DigitalInput;
import yams.core.mechanisms.config.SensorConfig;
import yams.core.motorcontrollers.simulation.Sensor;

private DigitalInput dio = new DigitalInput(0); // Standard DIO
private final Sensor coralSensor = new SensorConfig("CoralDetectorBeamBreak") // Name of the sensor 
  .withField("Beam", dio::get, false) // Add a Field to the sensor named "Beam" whose value is dio.get() and defaults to false
  .withSimulatedValue("Beam", Seconds.of(3), Seconds.of(4), true) // Change the "Beam" field to true between 3s and 4s into a match
  .withSimulatedValue("Beam",()->arm.isNear(Degrees.of(40), Degrees.of(2)), true) // Change "Beam" field to true when the arm is near 40deg +- 2deg
  .getSensor(); // Get the sensor.
```

You can use `int`, `long`, `double`, or `bool` as sensor field values.

## How do I add simulated values to a sensor?

You can add simulated values to an existing `Sensor` using `Sensor.addSimTrigger(field, value, trigger)` like below. The value is a `HALValue` (`org.wpilib.hardware.hal.HALValue`), for example `HALValue.makeBoolean(true)`.

<pre class="language-java"><code class="lang-java">private DigitalInput dio = new DigitalInput(0); // Standard DIO
private final Sensor coralSensor = new SensorConfig("CoralDetectorBeamBreak") // Name of the sensor 
  .withField("Beam", dio::get, false) // Add a Field to the sensor named "Beam" whose value is dio.get() and defaults to false
  .withSimulatedValue("Beam", Seconds.of(3), Seconds.of(4), true) // Change the "Beam" field to true between 3s and 4s into a match
  .getSensor(); // Get the sensor.

...
<strong>coralSensor.addSimTrigger("Beam", HALValue.makeBoolean(true), ()->arm.isNear(Degrees.of(40), Degrees.of(2))); // Change "Beam" field to true when the arm is near 40deg +- 2deg
</strong></code></pre>

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-4d892425b01ade12b02db8c6a0c4ab6d64fda9ab%2Fsensor-sim-values.gif?alt=media" alt="Animation of the Beam field of a beam break sensor in simulation, false by default. It turns true while the arm is within 2 degrees of 40 degrees, turns false when the arm moves on, turns true again while the match time is between 3 and 4 seconds, and then goes back to false."><figcaption><p>The field reads true while either simulated value is true, and goes back to what it was when neither is.</p></figcaption></figure>

## What does this look like?

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2FxZnE2jTxxjCEg91Q7rhP%2Fimage.png?alt=media&amp;token=bc957101-6b79-4283-a53b-a4e1069017aa" alt=""><figcaption></figcaption></figure>

The circled value changes when the Arm is near 40deg +-2 deg. You can also change it in the Simulation GUI manually.

## How do I use the value from a `Sensor`?

{% hint style="danger" %}
The sensor field value will only automatically update if the sensor is checked!
{% endhint %}

Instead of doing `dio.get()` in the example, you would have to do `coralSensor.getAsBoolean("Beam")` like the example below.

<pre class="language-java"><code class="lang-java">public boolean getBeamBreak()
{
  // return dio.get() // Will not work because the value is not modified by Sensor or automatically updated.
<strong>  return coralSensor.getAsBoolean("Beam");
</strong>}
</code></pre>

## How do I simulate the sensor value?

You can set the sensor value manually by getting the field (`.getField`) from a sensor and calling the `.set` function. It will overwrite the previous value of the sensor field until its changed from glass or via `.set` again.

{% hint style="info" %}
This will only change the sensor value when running under simulations. It does not affect the real value when running on the robot.
{% endhint %}

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Commands v2" %}

```java
public void setBeamBreakWhenArmIsLow()
{
    arm.lte(Degrees.of(10)).onTrue(Commands.runOnce(()->coralSensor.getField("Beam").set(true)));
}
```

{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Commands v3" %}

```java
public void setBeamBreakWhenArmIsLow()
{
    arm.lte(Degrees.of(10)).onTrue(Command.noRequirements(coroutine -> coralSensor.getField("Beam").set(true))
                                          .named("Set Beam Break"));
}
```

{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

## How do I simulate a sensor the mechanism triggers?

Some sensors are triggered by the mechanism itself, like a limit switch at a hard stop, or a beam break the arm passes through. Simulate those from where the simulated mechanism really is, rather than from what its encoder says. The motor's sim supplier (`SmartMotorController.getSimSupplier()`) has the simulated mechanism's position. It is empty on a real robot, so the condition is `false` there.

```java
private final DigitalInput lowerSwitchInput = new DigitalInput(4);
private final Sensor lowerSwitch = new SensorConfig("ArmLowerSwitch")
  .withField("Pressed", lowerSwitchInput::get, false)
  // Pressed while the simulated arm is within 0.05° of its lower hard stop at -30°.
  .withSimulatedValue("Pressed", () -> motor.getSimSupplier()
      .map(sim -> sim.getMechanismPosition().lte(Degrees.of(-29.95)))
      .orElse(false), true)
  .getSensor();
```

For an elevator, convert the simulated position to a height with the motor controller's config: `smcConfig.convertFromMechanism(sim.getMechanismPosition())`.

A real limit switch is pressed by the mechanism, not by its encoder. If the encoder is off, say after a belt skips, a switch simulated from the encoder (`arm.isNear(...)`) is pressed in the wrong place. One simulated from the sim supplier is pressed at the hard stop, as the real one is.

{% hint style="warning" %}
Declare the `Sensor` after `motor` (and the mechanism, if its lambda uses it). Java does not let a field's initializer use a field declared after it, even inside a lambda.
{% endhint %}

## How do I home a mechanism with a limit switch `Sensor`?

Set the motor's encoder to the hard stop each time the switch is pressed. A `Trigger` runs its `onTrue` command once each time the switch goes from released to pressed, and the scheduler reads the `Sensor` every loop, as its simulated values need.

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Commands v2" %}

```java
public boolean isLowerSwitchPressed()
{
  return lowerSwitch.getAsBoolean("Pressed");
}

public ArmSubsystem()
{
  new Trigger(this::isLowerSwitchPressed)
      .onTrue(Commands.runOnce(() -> motor.setEncoderPosition(Degrees.of(-30))).withName("Arm Home LowerSwitch"));
}
```

{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Commands v3" %}

```java
public boolean isLowerSwitchPressed()
{
  return lowerSwitch.getAsBoolean("Pressed");
}

public ArmMechanism()
{
  new Trigger(this::isLowerSwitchPressed)
      .onTrue(Command.noRequirements(coroutine -> motor.setEncoderPosition(Degrees.of(-30)))
                     .named("Arm Home LowerSwitch"));
}
```

{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

{% hint style="info" %}
A SystemCore Smart IO port has a pull-down resistor, unlike a roboRIO's pull-up. A normally open switch wired between the signal and power pins reads `true` while it is pressed, so its field is `input::get`, not `!input.get()`.
{% endhint %}

[YAMG](https://yamg.yassrobotics.com) generates all of this when you turn on **Homing**. For the wiring and the common pitfalls, see [How do I home a mechanism with limit switches?](/documentation/how-to-mechanisms/how-do-i-home-a-mechanism-with-limit-switches.md)

# Arms

An `Arm` is a mechanism that swings about a pivot and has to hold itself up against gravity: an intake arm, a shooter hood that tilts, a climber hook.

## How an arm works

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-afeaf6518bd6bf813e17b35d6216581bc8c83757%2Fmech-arm.png?alt=media" alt="An arm drawn at 40 degrees above a level dashed line marked 0 degrees, with straight up marked 90 degrees. Its length, set by withLength, runs from the pivot to the end of the arm, and gravity pulls down on the end. Level is zero because gravity pulls hardest on a level arm and ArmFeedforward uses kG times the cosine of the angle."><figcaption><p>An arm is measured from level.</p></figcaption></figure>

Following WPILib, an arm is at 0° when it is parallel to the ground and 90° when it points straight up. Gravity pulls hardest on a level arm and not at all on a vertical one, and [`ArmFeedforward`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/feedforward.md#withfeedforward) uses that angle to work out how much voltage holds the arm still. So the arm's encoder has to read 0 when the arm is level.

If your mechanism does not need gravity accounted for, because it turns about a vertical axis or is balanced, use a [Pivot](/documentation/understanding/turrets-wrists.md) instead.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-26a4cdaef46f02c8583bf1533b3272fcb1cdf544%2Fmech-flow.png?alt=media" alt="A flow diagram. A command such as arm.run or arm.runTo gives a setpoint to the mechanism, which gives it to the SmartMotorController. The controller runs the profile, PID, feedforward and limits and sends volts to the motor, and reads back the encoder position and velocity. updateTelemetry is called every loop to publish values and draw the mechanism. simIterate is called every loop in simulation to step the physics."><figcaption><p>The same chain runs every mechanism.</p></figcaption></figure>

A YAMS `Arm` is a thin layer over one [`SmartMotorController`](/documentation/understanding/editor.md). The `Arm` knows what the mechanism is, turns your commands into setpoints, and runs its simulation. The motor controller does the closed loop control. Keep one mechanism per subsystem, so live tuning and command requirements stay simple.

## Creating an arm

The code is the same for Commands v2 and Commands v3 except for the package you import from and the class that owns the mechanism.

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Commands v2" %}

```java
import yams.commands2.config.SmartMotorControllerConfig;
import yams.commands2.mechanisms.Arm;

public class ArmSubsystem extends SubsystemBase
{
  private final SmartMotorControllerConfig motorConfig = new SmartMotorControllerConfig(this)
      .withControlMode(ControlMode.CLOSED_LOOP)
      .withGearing(new MechanismGearing(GearBox.fromReductionStages(3, 4)))
      .withClosedLoopController(4, 0, 0)
      .withFeedforward(new ArmFeedforward(0, 0.3, 1.2))
      .withTrapezoidalProfile(DegreesPerSecond.of(180), DegreesPerSecondPerSecond.of(90))
      .withSoftLimits(Degrees.of(-30), Degrees.of(100))
      .withStartingPosition(Degrees.of(0))
      .withMomentOfInertia(Feet.of(3), Pounds.of(4))
      .withStatorCurrentLimit(Amps.of(40))
      .withZeroPower(MotorMode.BRAKE)
      .withTelemetry("ArmMotor", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);
  private final SmartMotorController motor = new SparkWrapper(spark, DCMotor.getNEO(1), motorConfig);
  private final ArmConfig config = new ArmConfig()
      .withLength(Feet.of(3))
      .withHardLimits(Degrees.of(-100), Degrees.of(200))
      .withTelemetry("Arm", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);
  private final Arm arm = new Arm(config, motor);

  @Override
  public void periodic()
  {
    arm.updateTelemetry(); // every loop
  }

  @Override
  public void simulationPeriodic()
  {
    arm.simIterate(); // every loop, in simulation
  }
}
```

`this` is the `Subsystem`. The scheduler calls `periodic()` and `simulationPeriodic()` for you.
{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Commands v3" %}

```java
import yams.commands3.config.SmartMotorControllerConfig;
import yams.commands3.mechanisms.Arm;

public class ArmMechanism implements Mechanism
{
  private final SmartMotorControllerConfig motorConfig = new SmartMotorControllerConfig(this)
      .withControlMode(ControlMode.CLOSED_LOOP)
      .withGearing(new MechanismGearing(GearBox.fromReductionStages(3, 4)))
      .withClosedLoopController(4, 0, 0)
      .withFeedforward(new ArmFeedforward(0, 0.3, 1.2))
      .withTrapezoidalProfile(DegreesPerSecond.of(180), DegreesPerSecondPerSecond.of(90))
      .withSoftLimits(Degrees.of(-30), Degrees.of(100))
      .withStartingPosition(Degrees.of(0))
      .withMomentOfInertia(Feet.of(3), Pounds.of(4))
      .withStatorCurrentLimit(Amps.of(40))
      .withZeroPower(MotorMode.BRAKE)
      .withTelemetry("ArmMotor", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);
  private final SmartMotorController motor = new SparkWrapper(spark, DCMotor.getNEO(1), motorConfig);
  private final ArmConfig config = new ArmConfig()
      .withLength(Feet.of(3))
      .withHardLimits(Degrees.of(-100), Degrees.of(200))
      .withTelemetry("Arm", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);
  private final Arm arm = new Arm(config, motor);

  public void periodic()
  {
    arm.updateTelemetry(); // every loop
  }

  public void simulationPeriodic()
  {
    arm.simIterate(); // every loop, in simulation
  }
}
```

`this` is the Commands v3 `Mechanism`. A `Mechanism` has no periodic hook, so call `periodic()` yourself from `Robot.robotPeriodic()`, and run `simulationPeriodic()` in its own `Notifier`, started from `Robot.simulationInit()`. [Where the tutorials put things](/documentation/tutorials/project-setup.md#where-the-tutorials-put-things) shows both.
{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

The options in `motorConfig` are explained in [SmartMotorControllerConfig](/documentation/understanding/editor/config.md), and the options in `config` in [ArmConfig](/documentation/understanding/arms/arm-config.md).

## Commands

Every command below exists with the same name in Commands v2 and Commands v3.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-3fadf4560c9eaad8dd912329ba84d272841fdef5%2Fmech-commands.png?alt=media" alt="Three timelines starting when the command is scheduled. run(setpoint), also called setAngle or setHeight, runs until something else takes the mechanism and holds the setpoint the whole time. runTo(setpoint, tolerance) ends when the mechanism is within the tolerance, and then the default command takes over. set(dutycycle) and setVoltage(volts) are open loop with no PID, run until interrupted, and the closed loop controller comes back when they end."><figcaption><p>When each kind of command ends.</p></figcaption></figure>

| Command                         | What it does                                       | Ends                                  |
| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `setAngle(Angle)`, `run(Angle)` | Go to the angle and hold it. The two are the same. | Never, until interrupted.             |
| `runTo(Angle, Angle tolerance)` | Go to the angle.                                   | When the arm is within the tolerance. |
| `set(double)`                   | Output a duty cycle, with the closed loop off.     | Never, until interrupted.             |
| `setVoltage(Voltage)`           | Output a voltage, with the closed loop off.        | Never, until interrupted.             |

`setAngle`, `run` and `runTo` also take a `Supplier<Angle>`, and `set` and `setVoltage` a supplier too, for a setpoint that changes while the command runs.

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Commands v2" %}

```java
// In the subsystem
public Command hold(Angle angle)
{
  return arm.setAngle(angle); // never ends: a good default command
}

public Command moveTo(Angle angle)
{
  return arm.runTo(angle, Degrees.of(2)); // ends when it gets there
}

// In RobotContainer
arm.setDefaultCommand(arm.hold(Degrees.of(0)));
Command score = arm.moveTo(Degrees.of(80)).andThen(intake.eject());
```

{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Commands v3" %}

```java
// In the mechanism
public Command hold(Angle angle)
{
  return run(coroutine -> coroutine.await(arm.setAngle(angle)))
      .withPriority(Command.LOWEST_PRIORITY) // so any other command can take over
      .named("Arm Hold");
}

public Command moveTo(Angle angle)
{
  return arm.runTo(angle, Degrees.of(2)); // ends when it gets there
}

// In Robot
arm.setDefaultCommand(arm.hold(Degrees.of(0)));
Command score = Command.noRequirements(coroutine -> {
  coroutine.await(arm.moveTo(Degrees.of(80)));
  coroutine.await(intake.eject());
}).named("Score");
```

{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

When a command ends the motor keeps its last request, so a mechanism that reached its goal stays there until another command, usually the default one, gives it something else. [run() vs runTo() Commands](/documentation/understanding/run-vs-runto.md) covers the pitfalls, including a default command undoing a `runTo()`.

## Setpoint functions

The commands above are built on plain functions that set the setpoint and return at once. Use them when you write your own command, or when something other than a command decides the setpoint, such as an AdvantageKit IO layer.

| Function                                   | What it does                                                                                                                                                                      |
| ------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `setMechanismPositionSetpoint(Angle)`      | Go to this angle with closed loop control.                                                                                                                                        |
| `setMeasurementPositionSetpoint(Distance)` | Go to this distance. Needs a [circumference](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/gearing-and-units.md#withmechanismcircumference).                                         |
| `setVoltageSetpoint(Voltage)`              | Output this voltage. Stops the closed loop.                                                                                                                                       |
| `setDutyCycleSetpoint(double)`             | Output this duty cycle, from -1 to 1. Stops the closed loop.                                                                                                                      |
| `getMechanismSetpoint()`                   | The current position setpoint as an angle, if there is one.                                                                                                                       |
| `getMeasurementSetpoint()`                 | The same setpoint as a distance. Empty if there is no setpoint or no [circumference](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/gearing-and-units.md#withmechanismcircumference). |

```java
arm.setMechanismPositionSetpoint(Degrees.of(45));
```

These do not require the subsystem or mechanism, so nothing stops two callers fighting over the setpoint. Prefer the commands unless you need these. [Setpoint Methods vs Command Helpers](/documentation/understanding/setpoint-methods.md) compares the two in detail.

## Asking where it is

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-eac0fa2da7a69e9234686a6b5e9d2363fe384615%2Fmech-triggers.png?alt=media" alt="A number line from minus 30 to 120 degrees with a mechanism at 72 degrees. near 80 within 10 is true. between 0 and 60 is false. gte 45 is true. lte 45 is false. max and min, at the ends of the travel, are false. Each is a Trigger, and the is functions such as isNear give the same answers as booleans."><figcaption><p>The same questions as Triggers or as booleans.</p></figcaption></figure>

| As a `Trigger`             | As a boolean                   | True when                                                                |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `near(angle, within)`      | `isNear(angle, within)`        | The arm is within `within` of `angle`.                                   |
| `between(start, end)`      | `isBetween(start, end)`        | The arm is between the two angles.                                       |
| `gte(angle)`, `lte(angle)` | `isGte(angle)`, `isLte(angle)` | The arm is at or above, or at or below, the angle.                       |
| `max()`, `min()`           | `isAtMax()`, `isAtMin()`       | The arm is at its soft limit, or its hard limit if no soft limit is set. |

`getAngle()` returns the arm's current angle.

```java
arm.near(Degrees.of(80), Degrees.of(2)).onTrue(intake.eject());
if (arm.isNear(Degrees.of(0), Degrees.of(2))) { /* stowed */ }
```

## Absolute encoders

An arm that does not know where it is when the robot turns on has to be started in the same place every time. An absolute encoder removes that.

{% hint style="warning" %}
YAMS reads an absolute encoder through the motor controller, so it must be one that motor controller supports: see [Encoders](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/encoders.md#withexternalencoder). For any other encoder, read it yourself and call `SmartMotorController.setEncoderPosition` once at startup.
{% endhint %}

[How do I use absolute encoders on my arm?](/documentation/how-to-mechanisms/how-do-i-use-absolute-encoders-on-my-arm.md) has a complete example for each vendor, including the [zero offset](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/encoders.md#withexternalencoderzerooffset) that makes the encoder read 0 when the arm is level.

## Arms with a brake

Some arms have a brake module that holds the arm so the motor can stop working. That saves battery and keeps the motor cool on an arm that holds a heavy load for a long time.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-d7fb19521fc68b4e57cbdde3bc3eb5b395e0410f%2Fmech-arm-brake.png?alt=media" alt="Four stages: when the motor drives, the brake lets go, by itself or on command; the arm moves with runTo; when the motor stops driving, the brake bites and holds the arm; then the motor output is zero, so it draws no current and makes no heat. A graph of motor current shows a spike while moving, a steady holding current until the brake bites, and then almost none, where an arm without a brake would draw holding current forever."><figcaption><p>The brake holds the arm once the motor stops driving it.</p></figcaption></figure>

{% hint style="info" %}
This is not `MotorMode.BRAKE`. [Brake mode](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/motor-basics.md#withzeropower) is electrical: it resists movement but cannot hold an arm up. A brake module is mechanical, and can.
{% endhint %}

Most brake modules are **automatic**: they bite on their own when the motor stops driving and let go when it drives again, with nothing for your code to command. Some are **manual**, released by a solenoid or a servo that your code controls. Check which yours is, because it changes what you can do. YAMS has no brake class of its own for either kind.

### What to aim for

These are suggestions, not rules. With an automatic brake you do not control when it bites, so you cannot always follow them. Get as close as your brake allows.

* **Let the brake do the holding.** Once the arm has arrived, stop driving the motor so the brake takes the load. An arm that keeps holding its setpoint against the brake gains nothing from having one.
* **Avoid driving against the brake.** A motor pushing on an engaged brake heats up and wears the brake. With an automatic brake some of this is unavoidable at the start of each move, while the brake lets go.
* **Avoid a default command that holds an angle.** It keeps the motor driving, which is the opposite of resting. A default command that rests the motor is ideal.
* **Allow for the brake's timing.** A brake takes a moment to bite and to let go. Expect a small lurch or sag at each end of a move, and allow for it where you can.

### Resting the motor

Resting means an output of zero with the closed loop controller stopped. If the closed loop kept running it would go on driving the arm to its setpoint, against the brake.

The `set(0)` helper command does all of it. It stops the closed loop controller, holds the output at zero, and starts the closed loop again when another command takes over. So you can ignore the closed loop entirely: make `set(0)` the arm's default command, and the arm rests whenever nothing else is moving it.

### With an automatic brake

There is nothing to command on the brake. Move with `runTo`, which ends when the arm arrives, and the default command rests the motor so the brake bites:

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Commands v2" %}

```java
// In the subsystem
public Command rest()
{
  return arm.set(0); // output 0, closed loop stopped; it restarts when another command takes over
}

public Command moveTo(Angle angle)
{
  return arm.runTo(angle, Degrees.of(2)); // driving releases the brake
}

// In RobotContainer
arm.setDefaultCommand(arm.rest());
```

{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Commands v3" %}

```java
// In the mechanism
public Command rest()
{
  return run(coroutine -> coroutine.await(arm.set(0))) // output 0, closed loop stopped
      .withPriority(Command.LOWEST_PRIORITY)           // so any other command can take over
      .named("Arm Rest");
}

public Command moveTo(Angle angle)
{
  return arm.runTo(angle, Degrees.of(2)); // driving releases the brake
}

// In Robot
arm.setDefaultCommand(arm.rest());
```

{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

### With a manual brake

If your code controls the brake, release it before the move and engage it after. The default command still rests the motor:

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Commands v2" %}

```java
// brake is your own actuator: a Solenoid, a Servo, or anything with an on and off
public Command moveTo(Angle angle)
{
  return runOnce(() -> brake.set(false))         // release
      .andThen(arm.runTo(angle, Degrees.of(2)))  // move
      .finallyDo(() -> brake.set(true));         // engage, even if interrupted
}

// In RobotContainer
arm.setDefaultCommand(arm.rest());               // then the motor rests
```

{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Commands v3" %}

```java
// brake is your own actuator: a Solenoid, a Servo, or anything with an on and off
public Command moveTo(Angle angle)
{
  return run(coroutine -> {
    brake.set(false);                                  // release
    coroutine.await(arm.runTo(angle, Degrees.of(2)));  // move
    brake.set(true);                                   // engage
  }).whenCanceled(() -> brake.set(true)).named("Arm Move And Brake");
}

// In Robot
arm.setDefaultCommand(arm.rest());                     // then the motor rests
```

{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

{% hint style="info" %}
Resting the motor with `set(0)` or `arm.setDutyCycleSetpoint(0)` stops a closed loop that runs on the SystemCore. The next move, with a command such as `runTo(...)` or with `arm.setMechanismPositionSetpoint(...)`, starts it again for you. Only when you call `arm.getMotorController()` directly do you start and stop it yourself, see [Starting and stopping the SystemCore closed loop](/documentation/understanding/setpoint-methods.md#starting-and-stopping-the-systemcore-closed-loop).
{% endhint %}

Soft limits still apply while the arm moves, with either kind of brake. The brake does not replace them.

## Configuring and tuning an arm, step by step

Do the configuration steps first, in simulation, then the tuning steps on the real mechanism with [live tuning](/documentation/understanding/live-tuning.md). Tuning something that is configured wrong wastes the time.

{% hint style="danger" %}
Like all tuning, this can be dangerous. Keep clear of the mechanism, start with low current limits and small moves, and practice in simulation first.
{% endhint %}

### Configure it

{% stepper %}
{% step %}

#### Set the gearing

Give the reduction from the motor to the arm's pivot with [`withGearing`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/gearing-and-units.md#withgearing). Check it by moving the arm a known angle by hand and reading `mechanism/position`.
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Make up positive

Apply a small positive voltage with `setVoltage`. If the arm goes down, flip [`withMotorInverted`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/motor-basics.md#withmotorinverted). If the angle counts down while it goes up, an [encoder is backwards](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/encoders.md#withencoderinverted).
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Make level read zero

Hold the arm level. With an absolute encoder, set its [zero offset](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/encoders.md#withexternalencoderzerooffset) so it reads 0 there. Without one, set a [starting position](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/encoders.md#withstartingposition) for where the arm rests when the robot turns on.
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Set the limits

Put [`withHardLimits`](/documentation/understanding/mechanism-configs/shared-options.md#withhardlimits) where the stops are and [`withSoftLimits`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/limits-and-protection.md#withsoftlimits) a few degrees inside them.
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Protect the motor

Set a low [stator current limit](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/limits-and-protection.md#current-limits) to start with, and `MotorMode.BRAKE`.
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Describe it to the simulator

Set [`withLength`](/documentation/understanding/arms/arm-config.md#withlength) and [`withMomentOfInertia`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/simulation.md#withmomentofinertia), run it in simulation, and check the arm moves the way you expect before enabling the real one.
{% endstep %}
{% endstepper %}

### Tune it

{% stepper %}
{% step %}

#### Find kG with the arm level

Set kP, kI, kD and the other feedforward gains to 0. Hold the arm level and raise `closedloop/feedforward/kG` until the arm stays where you leave it. Level is where gravity pulls hardest, so a kG found there is right at every angle.
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Find kS

Raise `closedloop/feedforward/kS` until the arm is just about to move, then back off a little.
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Raise kP, then add kD

Raise kP until the arm reaches the setpoint quickly, and stop when it starts to shake. Add a little kD if it overshoots. Without a profile a new setpoint is a sudden jump, so tune with small moves, a few degrees or centimeters at a time.
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Add the motion profile last

Once the arm holds and reaches small setpoints cleanly, set a maximum velocity and acceleration the arm can really reach, and raise them a step at a time. If the arm cannot keep up, the profile is too fast, not the gains too low.
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Find kV with the profile

kV only acts while the setpoint is moving, so it is tuned with the profile. Run a long move and watch the setpoint velocity against `mechanism/velocity` in the middle of it. Raise kV until they match.
{% endstep %}

{% step %}

#### Check it everywhere

Test near both limits, straight up, level, and holding a game piece. If the loaded arm needs different gains, put them in a second [slot](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/closed-loop-control.md#slots). [How do I control a mechanism that carries a game piece?](/documentation/how-to-mechanisms/how-do-i-control-a-mechanism-that-carries-a-game-piece.md) explains how much the gains change, and how to switch them.
{% endstep %}
{% endstepper %}

[Tuning order](/documentation/understanding/live-tuning.md#tuning-order) has the same steps with the NetworkTables keys to watch.

### Tuning an arm with a brake

Tune the arm itself with the steps above first. If your brake can be held off, hold it off while you do. If it is automatic and cannot be, tune with it in place and expect the gains to carry a little of its drag.

Then look at how the arm and the brake work together. These are things to check and try, not steps that every brake allows:

* **Watch the start of a move.** A lurch or a spike in `current/stator` at the start is the motor pushing on a brake that has not let go yet. A slower start to the [profile](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/motion-profiles.md#withtrapezoidalprofile) gives an automatic brake time to release. With a manual brake, release it a moment earlier.
* **Watch the end of a move.** If `mechanism/position` sags as the brake bites, the motor let go before the brake held. With a manual brake, engage it before resting the motor. With an automatic one, a sag of a known size can be allowed for in the setpoint.
* **Check the rest.** With the arm braked, `current/stator` should be close to zero. If it is not, something is still commanding the motor: look for a default command that holds a setpoint.
* **Widen the tolerance if it hunts.** A brake holds wherever it bites. If `runTo` takes a long time to finish, the last fraction of a degree is not worth the wait.
* **Keep an eye on `motor/temperature`** over a few minutes of use. If the motor still runs hot, it is working against the brake somewhere.

