# Elevators

An `Elevator` moves a carriage along a line: a lift, a telescoping climber, or a linear slide.

## How an elevator works

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-754589ffb47c57a8684ab7cacfb3b4cbfd7a1178%2Fmech-elevator-how.png?alt=media" alt="A motor drives a gearbox, set by withGearing, which turns a drum or sprocket, set by withDrumRadius, which pulls a carriage up a rail. One drum rotation lifts the carriage one circumference, so the height is the drum rotations times the circumference and you work in meters."><figcaption><p>A rotating motor becomes a distance.</p></figcaption></figure>

The motor turns a drum or sprocket through a gearbox, and the drum pulls the carriage. One rotation of the drum moves the carriage one circumference, so once YAMS knows the [drum size](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/gearing-and-units.md#withmechanismcircumference) you can work in meters and never think about rotations. [How do I find my mechanism's circumference?](/documentation/how-to-motor-controllers-and-sensors/how-to-find-your-mechanism-circumference.md) covers measuring it.

A vertical elevator has to hold its carriage up, which is what the `kG` term of [`ElevatorFeedforward`](/documentation/understanding/editor/config/feedforward.md#withfeedforward) is for.

<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-26a4cdaef46f02c8583bf1533b3272fcb1cdf544%2Fmech-flow.png?alt=media" alt="A flow diagram. A command such as arm.run or arm.runTo gives a setpoint to the mechanism, which gives it to the SmartMotorController. The controller runs the profile, PID, feedforward and limits and sends volts to the motor, and reads back the encoder position and velocity. updateTelemetry is called every loop to publish values and draw the mechanism. simIterate is called every loop in simulation to step the physics."><figcaption><p>The same chain runs every mechanism.</p></figcaption></figure>

A YAMS `Elevator` is a thin layer over one [`SmartMotorController`](/documentation/understanding/editor.md). The `Elevator` knows what the mechanism is, turns your commands into setpoints, and runs its simulation. The motor controller does the closed loop control. Keep one mechanism per subsystem, so live tuning and command requirements stay simple.

## Creating an elevator

The code is the same for Commands v2 and Commands v3 except for the package you import from and the class that owns the mechanism.

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Commands v2" %}

```java
import yams.commands2.config.SmartMotorControllerConfig;
import yams.commands2.mechanisms.Elevator;

public class ElevatorSubsystem extends SubsystemBase
{
  private final SmartMotorControllerConfig motorConfig = new SmartMotorControllerConfig(this)
      .withControlMode(ControlMode.CLOSED_LOOP)
      .withGearing(new MechanismGearing(GearBox.fromReductionStages(3, 4)))
      .withDrumRadius(Inches.of(0.25), 22) // chain pitch and sprocket teeth
      .withClosedLoopController(4, 0, 0)
      .withFeedforward(new ElevatorFeedforward(0, 0.4, 3.5))
      .withTrapezoidalProfile(MetersPerSecond.of(1.5), MetersPerSecondPerSecond.of(3))
      .withSoftLimits(Meters.of(0), Meters.of(1.25))
      .withStartingPosition(Meters.of(0))
      .withStatorCurrentLimit(Amps.of(40))
      .withZeroPower(MotorMode.BRAKE)
      .withTelemetry("ElevatorMotor", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);
  private final SmartMotorController motor = new SparkWrapper(spark, DCMotor.getNEO(1), motorConfig);
  private final ElevatorConfig config = new ElevatorConfig()
      .withCarriageWeight(Pounds.of(16))
      .withHardLimits(Meters.of(0), Meters.of(1.3))
      .withTelemetry("Elevator", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);
  private final Elevator elevator = new Elevator(config, motor);

  @Override
  public void periodic()
  {
    elevator.updateTelemetry(); // every loop
  }

  @Override
  public void simulationPeriodic()
  {
    elevator.simIterate(); // every loop, in simulation
  }
}
```

`this` is the `Subsystem`. The scheduler calls `periodic()` and `simulationPeriodic()` for you.
{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Commands v3" %}

```java
import yams.commands3.config.SmartMotorControllerConfig;
import yams.commands3.mechanisms.Elevator;

public class ElevatorMechanism implements Mechanism
{
  private final SmartMotorControllerConfig motorConfig = new SmartMotorControllerConfig(this)
      .withControlMode(ControlMode.CLOSED_LOOP)
      .withGearing(new MechanismGearing(GearBox.fromReductionStages(3, 4)))
      .withDrumRadius(Inches.of(0.25), 22) // chain pitch and sprocket teeth
      .withClosedLoopController(4, 0, 0)
      .withFeedforward(new ElevatorFeedforward(0, 0.4, 3.5))
      .withTrapezoidalProfile(MetersPerSecond.of(1.5), MetersPerSecondPerSecond.of(3))
      .withSoftLimits(Meters.of(0), Meters.of(1.25))
      .withStartingPosition(Meters.of(0))
      .withStatorCurrentLimit(Amps.of(40))
      .withZeroPower(MotorMode.BRAKE)
      .withTelemetry("ElevatorMotor", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);
  private final SmartMotorController motor = new SparkWrapper(spark, DCMotor.getNEO(1), motorConfig);
  private final ElevatorConfig config = new ElevatorConfig()
      .withCarriageWeight(Pounds.of(16))
      .withHardLimits(Meters.of(0), Meters.of(1.3))
      .withTelemetry("Elevator", TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH);
  private final Elevator elevator = new Elevator(config, motor);

  public void periodic()
