<figure><img src="https://1354553280-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-x-prod.appspot.com/o/spaces%2FZM0CFmYiQzcrY4zDcTtZ%2Fuploads%2Fgit-blob-4fd1a23676ccee5b767a1f0f343b9aa5b8ae5098%2Fsetpoint-decision.png?alt=media" alt="A decision diagram. If the mechanism is controlled by a button binding or an autonomous sequence, use a command helper such as arm.run, arm.runTo or arm.setAngle. If it is controlled by a custom command, an IO class or a state machine, use a direct setpoint method such as arm.setMechanismPositionSetpoint, elevator.setMeasurementPositionSetpoint or arm.setVoltageSetpoint."><figcaption><p>Start from what is making the call.</p></figcaption></figure>

## Best practices

1. **Prefer Command Helpers** - They handle subsystem requirements and closed loop lifecycle
2. **Use run() for Teleop** - Continuous control that responds to interruption
3. **Use runTo() for Sequences** - When you need to wait for the mechanism to reach a target
4. **Use Direct Methods for IO** - When implementing AdvantageKit IO or custom commands
5. **Always Check Support** - Verify the method is supported before using direct setpoint methods
6. **Use Triggers for State** - Use `near()`, `max()`, `min()` triggers instead of polling

